\description{
\code{forceatlas2_large()} and \code{layout_forceatlas2_large()} compute a
ForceAtlas2 layout without leaving the graphics card (GPU): the graph is
sent to the card once, every step of every iteration runs there, and the
positions are fetched at the end. They are meant for graphs of hundreds of
thousands to millions of nodes, and need a GPU (see \code{\link[=gpu_devices]{gpu_devices()}}).

\code{forceatlas2_large()} returns the graph with the coordinates added to its
nodes, like \code{\link[=forceatlas2]{forceatlas2()}}, and \code{layout_forceatlas2_large()} returns the
matrix of coordinates, like \code{\link[=layout_forceatlas2]{layout_forceatlas2()}}.
}
\details{
The forces, the adaptive speed and the settings are those of
\code{\link[=forceatlas2]{forceatlas2()}}. What differs is how the repulsion is approximated.
\code{\link[=forceatlas2]{forceatlas2()}} and \code{\link[=forceatlas2_gpu]{forceatlas2_gpu()}} build, on the CPU, the tree of
regions that Gephi uses. Here the tree is built on the GPU at every
iteration with a method made for it: the nodes are sorted along a
space-filling curve (Morton order) and a binary tree is derived from the
sorted nodes, all in parallel. The repulsion is then the usual Barnes-Hut
one: a region acts as a single node when it is far enough, that is when
\code{distance * theta > size}. The size of a region is here the diagonal of
its bounding box times the square root of two, chosen so that a given
\code{theta} is about as precise as with the other functions.

As a consequence:
\itemize{
\item the layouts have the same properties as those of the other functions but
are not identical to them, since the approximation is different (with
\code{theta = 0} nothing is approximated, and the result is the exact one);
\item the repulsion is always approximated: there is no \code{barnes_hut} argument;
\item for a given graph, starting point and device, the result is reproducible.
}

With \code{precision = "single"} (the default), the positions are also stored in
single precision, i.e. with about seven significant digits. This is fine
for drawing a graph. Use \code{precision = "double"} if you need more, knowing
that consumer cards are much slower in double precision.

The memory used on the card is about 180 bytes per node and 16 bytes per
edge in single precision (twice as much for most of it in double
precision): roughly 2 GB for ten million nodes and fifty million edges.

Graphs with fewer than two nodes are handled on the CPU. For small graphs
(a few thousand nodes) these functions work but are slower than
\code{\link[=forceatlas2]{forceatlas2()}}, because of the fixed cost of driving the card.

With \code{options(forceatlas2r.timings = TRUE)}, the matrix returned by
\code{layout_forceatlas2_large()} has a \code{"timings"} attribute giving, in
seconds, the total time and the time spent in each phase of the
iterations (measuring them slows the layout down a little).
}
